Method overview



Field
Entry




Family / class
Biomass dynamic models → unstructured biomass dynamics


Method name
SPiCT (R/TMB) — DTUAqua/spict


Short description
State-space surplus production model in continuous time, fitted by maximum likelihood with random effects integrated by the Laplace approximation (TMB).


Main outputs
Biomass and F trajectories, catch and index fits, production curve, \(B/B_{MSY}\), \(F/F_{MSY}\) with confidence intervals, forecasts.


Typical use case
Catch + one or more indices; fast dynamics, seasonal or irregularly timed data; repeated sensitivity runs.



SPiCT, the Stochastic Production Model in Continuous Time, is a state-space surplus production model implemented in R using Template Model Builder (TMB). Its continuous-time formulation is a key distinction from annual discrete-time SPMs. This can be useful for shorter-lived species, seasonal fisheries, and cases where catches or indices occur at irregular times.
SPiCT estimates latent biomass and fishing-mortality trajectories while accounting for stochastic process variation and observation error. The model is fitted by maximum likelihood, with random effects integrated using the Laplace approximation.

Conceptual strengths
SPiCT is particularly useful where:

the stock has relatively fast dynamics;
data include seasonal or irregular timing;
analysts need fast fitting and repeated sensitivity runs;
a likelihood-based framework is preferred;
continuous-time biomass and fishing-mortality dynamics are advantageous;
diagnostics and forecast functions are required in a reproducible workflow.



Outputs to emphasise
The SPiCT chapter should emphasize:

biomass trajectory;
fishing mortality or harvest-rate trajectory;
catch and index fits;
production-function plot;
relative stock status \(B/B_{MSY}\) and \(F/F_{MSY}\);
uncertainty intervals;
residual diagnostics;
retrospective analysis;
hindcast cross-validation;
sensitivity tables.

A SPiCT Kobe-style plot can be included for descriptive status communication, but it should not be described as posterior probability output.

Synthesis
SPiCT and JABBA have developed from methodological frameworks into state-space assessment tools used across several advisory contexts, including:

ICES advisory systems;
tuna RFMOs;
NOAA benchmark assessments;
Mediterranean mixed-fishery systems;
national assessment systems;
Pacific pelagic and squid assessments;
Asian comparative TAC frameworks.

SPiCT has documented implementation within ICES and GFCM and is increasingly documented in Pacific and South American systems. JABBA has documented use within ICCAT, IOTC, NOAA and selected national applications, including Brazil.
The use of uncertainty-grid approaches, model weighting, retrospective filtering and ensemble-style inference indicates a broader transition towards risk-based and structurally explicit surplus production modelling workflows.
The catalogue should be viewed as a working reference list of documented applications and as a basis for further linkage with the FAO global assessment database.

From training to assessment
Apps are well suited to learning a method and to first screening of a stock. Before results are used for advice, the analysis should meet the same standard as a scripted one:

the data preparation steps have been done on the real data;
the method’s misuse prevention checks pass;
the diagnostics and sensitivity runs are reported;
the settings and data are saved, so that the run can be repeated, ideally as an R script.

